# Disease Background
Hemophilia B is an inherited bleeding disorder characterized by a deficiency or abnormal function of coagulation Factor IX (FIX), 
a protein that plays an important role in the blood-clotting process. It is also known as Christmas disease and is considered the second 
most common type of hemophilia, accounting for approximately 15–20% of hemophilia cases. Hemophilia B primarily affects males because the 
gene responsible for the production of Factor IX is located on the X chromosome.
The genetic cause of Hemophilia B is associated with mutations in the F9 gene, which is located on the long arm of the X chromosome at Xq27.1. 
The F9 gene provides the instructions for producing coagulation Factor IX. Mutations in this gene can result in reduced production or impaired 
function of Factor IX, leading to an inability of the blood to form a stable clot efficiently. Different types of pathogenic variants, including 
missense, nonsense, splice-site, insertion, deletion, and promoter variants, have been identified in the F9 gene. The type and effect of the mutation 
can influence the amount of functional Factor IX available in the blood and, consequently, the severity of the disorder.

#F9 Gene and Normal Protein
The F9 (coagulation factor IX) gene provides instructions for making factor IX (FIX), a blood-clotting protein mainly produced in the liver. 
Factor IX plays an important role in the blood coagulation process, where it helps activate factor X and 
contributes to the formation of a stable blood clot. 

